When determining whether a reasonable person would conclude that an access or disclosure of personal information is likely or not likely to result in serious harm, the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) requires consideration of several matters. These include:

- The sensitivity and nature of the information;
- Whether the information has been disclosed to another party;
- Any other relevant circumstances.

The Privacy Commissioner may also consider whether there are reasonable steps that could have been taken by the individual or entity holding the information to prevent the disclosure, as well as any measures in place to protect the information. [Source: s 6]

A statement about an eligible data breach under the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) of Australia must include:

- The nature and extent of the breach;
- The identity of the individual whose personal information was affected by the breach, if known; 
- Details of how the individual can access their personal information that may have been compromised; 
- Any steps taken or proposed to be taken to mitigate any harm caused by the breach.

This information is required under s 6(1) of the Act.
